[1] Ann (declared) · to Ada, Ash
    Please read docs/passage.md in your working directory. Ada: recover the argument — state its premises and its conclusion exactly as the passage gives them. Ash: test the passage's claim on one concrete example of your own choosing, with numbers, and say whether the example supports step 3. Keep your answer under 150 words.

[2] Ada (verified from agent-session/pf-ada) · reply→1@r5 · to Ann
    **Ada — recovery of the argument (docs/passage.md)**
    Premise 1 — To cross any distance, a traveller must first cross half of it.
    Premise 2 — After that, half of what remains, and again half of what remains, without end.
    Premise 3 — A task made of endlessly many steps, each requiring some time, can never be completed.
    Conclusion (4) — Therefore the traveller never arrives: motion across a distance is impossible.
    Structure: 1 and 2 together make crossing an endless sequence of half-steps; 3 rules out ever finishing such a sequence; 4 follows that arrival — hence motion — is impossible. The load-bearing premise is 3: it quietly assumes that infinitely many steps must take infinite time, which is exactly where Ash's numerical test should press.

[3] Ash (verified from agent-session/pf-ash) · reply→1@r5 · to Ann
    I'll take the 2-metre walk to the door at a steady 1 m/s. The steps are 1 m, then 0.5 m, then 0.25 m, 0.125 m… — endlessly many, exactly as step 2 says. At constant speed their durations are 1 s, 0.5 s, 0.25 s, 0.125 s… The total time is 1 + ½ + ¼ + … = 2 s. You're at the door in two seconds.
    So my example does not support step 3. Step 3 needs a hidden extra premise — that endlessly many steps must take endlessly much time, e.g. that each step costs the same duration. But if each successive step is half the distance, it needs half the time, and a geometric series of durations sums to a finite total. Endlessly many steps, finite time: step 3 is false, and step 4 loses its ground.

[4] Ann (declared) · branch→2@r7 · to Ada
